[Music]  Olympic athletes defy gravity and  impress audiences around the world but  many struggle financially on the road to  those games the US Olympic Committee a  nonprofit reported 339 million dollars  in revenue back in 2016 and the  International Olympic Committee makes  billions of dollars from broadcasting  rights and sponsorship deals Dana  Jacobson is in Pyeongchang South Korea  with a look at the financial burden at  some of the athletes face in pursuit of  their Olympic dreams Dana I hear it's  costly a good morning good morning that  it is an Olympic athlete will tell you  you can't put a price tag on the Olympic  experience but there is a cost to  getting here take those who have to  crowdfund online just to pay for some of  their training or to get their family to  Pyeongchang now there is a small group  of athletes who get rich in their quest  for Olympic glory but for others there  is a harsh reality and it's like driving  a car on oil on oil really fast really  fast with guardrails around for Team USA  loser chris master riding really fast  has paid off here in Pyeongchang it's  awesome to say we did it and thank you  he won silver in individual men's luge  becoming the first American man to ever  medal in the event but it almost didn't  happen you a couple weeks ago thought I  know I'd have to get out of the sport  exactly I wanted to stay into it for  another four years but the thought of  staying in the sport without being able  to make anything or to be able to give  back just even pay rent is it's a really  serious thing that people don't know  exists while he savors the victory he  remembers the struggle I was a bartender  work banquets restaurants so it's a  fight to get to that level but if you're  as committed like you'll do anything how  big of a disparity is there between  Winter Olympic athletes  well huge Patrick Quinn is an agent  representing several athletes at the  games when you talk about an extreme of  a Lindsey Vonn and a Sean Whyte and the  vast majority of the Olympians  it's a whole different world it's first  class to coach for sure unlike many  other countries the US federal  government does not fund Olympic  programs in 1978 Congress turned over  the job of representing American  athletes to the US Olympic Committee a  non-profit loser Chris Mader has been  successful enough to get free housing in  an Olympic Training Center you have to  get to that level but you're not really  making any money the USOC doles out  money using a pay-for-performance model  athletes and sports that are most likely  to win medals get the most money coming  from a small sport I think when it does  come down to the finances yeah there is  a little bit of jealousy just because  you feel like you're putting in a  similar amount of effort but you don't  receive the same back and I think the  reason you stay in lose is because  you're passionate about it in its latest  annual report the USOC says ninety-three  percent of its revenue goes to support  Olympic and Paralympic athletes 2008  Olympian van Barger a former member of  the USO sees athletes Advisory Council  estimates that just six percent reaches  athletes pockets in the form of direct  payments when asked if this figure was  correct the USOC  said no but would not provide another  number Barger told CBS News the US  Congress should review what he calls the  financial strategy and waste of the USOC  for athletes who fund their own way  sponsors can be a big source of  financial help but the IOC s rule 40  puts restrictions on brands that don't  also sponsor the games you can't use the  word Olympic you can't use gold or  silver 2018 how new athletes work their  sponsors if they're not Olympic sponsors  it's challenging and gets complicated  use this word and you can't do it at  this time and that sort of thing while  the rules have been relaxed a US ski  jumper tweeted his frustration saying  the sponsorship ban makes it quote hard  for the little guy to make money or even  just support themselves  I do compliment the USOC they do a  tremendous amount of bringing sponsors  in to support as many athletes  but the reality is they can't fund all  of the athletes that need the funding  and so some of the rules have actually  been relaxed as for loozer chris master  he's pondering a future as a financial  planner but he's not giving up his sled  just yet a few weeks ago you didn't know  if you would continue financially what  does that silver medal mean now this  silver medal right here says I'm going  another four years and it also gives me  the ability first off to say thank you  but to be able to give back because I  understand the struggle I've been there  for many many years master said that  while he wasn't always rewarded  financially for his sport he was paid in  life experience things he would not have  gotten to do otherwise and as he moves  forward he'll have a little help along  the way Patrick Quinn the agent who we  spoke with will now be working with him  we should point out that we reached out  to the International Olympic Committee  for comment on our story they referred  us to the USOC John Danny Jacobsen and  Jung Chang a really interesting story  yeah it seems like they should be able  to make some kind of a living while  trying to compete for the United States  of America especially because it's a  dream can you imagine that you know you  have the talent you just want somebody  to believe in you please send money well  maybe now more well yeah

